    <div>
      <h2>Depression as an adaptation, not a defect</h2>
      <p>Flatness is often what is left after a system has spent years managing something it
      could not change. Shutting down was the available option once, and it worked. NARM starts
      from that question rather than from diagnosis: where did I learn this, how was it serving
      me, is it still?</p>
      <p>That matters because the usual instruction, try harder and do more, lands on the part
      of you that already believes the problem is a lack of effort. Self-criticism is not the
      cure for depression; it is frequently one of its engines.</p>
      <p>Where the flatness sits on top of developmental trauma, the work looks like
      <a href="/therapy/trauma/">the trauma page</a>. Where it alternates with a body that will
      not settle, see <a href="/therapy/anxiety/">anxiety</a>.</p>
      <h3>What people bring here</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Flatness rather than sadness, and guilt about not being sadder.</li>
        <li>Doing the things and feeling nothing about any of them.</li>
        <li>Procrastination that is not laziness and does not respond to discipline.</li>
        <li>A sense that something is off that you cannot point at.</li>
        <li>Functioning well in public and collapsing in private.</li>
        <li>Life transitions that took the floor out: graduating, leaving, arriving, becoming a
        parent, losing someone.</li>
        <li>Self care for mothers, which usually starts with permission.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>On procrastination</h3>
      <p>Procrastination that survives every productivity system is rarely about time
      management. More often a part of you is holding the brake for a reason, and the reason is
      worth knowing before it gets overridden. IFS-informed work gets to know the protector
      rather than pushing past it.</p>
      <h3>Pacing</h3>
      <p>Stabilisation comes first: acute stress, ongoing stress, feeling overwhelmed, loss.
      Only with that footing does processing work become useful rather than destabilising.</p>
      <h3>Reem's credentials</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>NARM therapist and Somatic Experiencing Practitioner (SEP)</li>
        <li>EMDR certified and an EMDRIA Approved Consultant</li>
        <li>IFS-informed EMDR, steps one through three completed in 2025</li>
        <li>LPC-S in Texas since 2009, licence 66562, over fifteen years in practice</li>
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/about/">Reem's full background</a></p>
    </div>
